Q: Can&#39;t access all class members in QVariantList from QML A: <p>A few things to note here:</p>
<p><strong>You only need to register a type, if you plan on instantiating it in QML</strong><br />
I.e. as long as you do not plan on writing something like:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>ConfigurationAmbient {
  ...        
}
</code></pre>
<p>in QML, you can remove both</p>
<pre><code>qmlRegisterType&lt;Configuration::AmbientLoopConfiguration&gt;(&quot;smah.configuration.ambient&quot;, 1, 0, &quot;ConfigurationAmbient&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>as well as <code>import smah.configuration.ambient</code>. The same goes for the <code>Configuration</code> object.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>You do not provide meta property access for your struct members.</strong><br />
Whilst you can wrap objects with variants to pass them around in QML, if you want to <em>access</em> the types members, you need to provide the necessary infrastructure (i.e. the Q_PROPERTY macro).</p>
<p>This is not inherited to nested types, so the fact that <code>Configuration</code> is a QObject does not make <code>AmbientLoopConfiguration</code> a QObject, too. You'll need to make that explicit.</p>
<p>In your case, this may look something like this (note: <code>Q_GADGET</code> removes the need to inherit from QObject for simple types like this):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct AmbientLoopConfiguration {
    Q_GADGET
    Q_PROPERTY(QString mName MEMBER mName);
    Q_PROPERTY(QString mFile MEMBER mFile);
    QString mName = &quot;foo&quot;;
    QString mFile = &quot;bar&quot;;
};
</code></pre>
<p>Which brings us to another issue:</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>You cannot declare nested meta types</strong></p>
<p>If you keep <code>AmbientLoopConfiguration</code> as a nested type of <code>Configuration</code>, you won't be able to write what I have shown in the paragraph above. <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/32126809/inner-classes-in-qt">Your moc will yell at you</a>:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>error: Meta object features not supported for nested classes</p>
</blockquote>
<p>To fix that, move the struct outside the class, possibly into its own header file.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong>Alternatively, convert to a variant map</strong><br />
Another option you have, would be to convert your struct into <a href="https://doc.qt.io/qt-6/qtqml-cppintegration-data.html#qvariantlist-and-qvariantmap-to-javascript-array-like-and-object" rel="nofollow noreferrer">something the QML type system already understands</a>, when passing it in your getter - which would be a QVariantMap:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>struct AmbientLoopConfiguration {
    QString mName = &quot;foo&quot;;
    QString mFile = &quot;bar&quot;;

    QVariantMap toMap() {
        return {
            { &quot;mName&quot;, mName },
            { &quot;mFile&quot;, mFile }
        };
    }
};

...

QVariantList getAmbientLoopConfigurations() {
    QVariantList l;
    for (Configuration::AmbientLoopConfiguration &amp;c : this-&gt;config.mAmbientLoopConfigurations) {
        l.append(c.toMap());
    }
    return l;
};
</code></pre>
<p>This has the following advantages:</p>
<ul>
<li>you can keep your struct nested in your class</li>
<li>You do not need to define <code>Q_PROPERTY</code> members</li>
<li>You can still use a property style access to the elements of the map - so this will work as you expect: <code>console.log(configuration.getAmbientLoopConfigurations[0].mName)</code></li>
</ul>
<p>But also some drawbacks:</p>
<ul>
<li>You need to manually create the conversion function</li>
<li>You will lose the type identity (<code>console.log(configuration.getAmbientLoopConfigurations[0])</code> now prints <code>qml: [object V4ReferenceObject]</code>)</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p><strong>Which solution is better depends on your greater use case.</strong></p>
<p>Personally, I'd favor the first approach (<code>Q_GADGET</code>) over converting to a <code>QVariantMap</code> in most cases. It is more explicit in what it tries to do and you can easily pass the object back from QML to C++ without losing information or having to write extensive conversions.</p>
<p>However, if the only purpose is to provide a bit of data to display in your UI, having a simple map to pass around may be perfectly fine and comes with the (possible) advantage of keeping the <code>Configuration</code> and <code>AmbientLoopConfiguration</code> tightly related.</p>

Q: PHP Codeigniter url routing: token for registering process in the url A: <p>what you want to do is, after user successfully registered, show him/her a url to complete the registration right?</p>

<p>As far as I understood the problem, you can do like this, </p>

<p>in your controller,</p>

<pre><code>$message = '';                     
$message .= '&lt;strong&gt;You have signed up with our website&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;';
$message .= '&lt;strong&gt;Please click:&lt;/strong&gt; ' . $link;

$data = array(
               'message' =&gt; $message 
          );

$this-&gt;load-&gt;view('header');
$this-&gt;load-&gt;view('complete', $data);
$this-&gt;load-&gt;view('footer'); 
</code></pre>

<p>And create a view called <em>complete.php</em>  </p>

<pre><code>&lt;html&gt;
&lt;head&gt;
&lt;title&gt;&lt;/title&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1&gt;&lt;?php echo $message;?&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt; 
</code></pre>

<p>Adding Dynamic Data to the View - <a href="https://www.codeigniter.com/userguide2/general/views.html" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://www.codeigniter.com/userguide2/general/views.html</a>                    </p>
